    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-t/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A box contains 5 balls of which 2 are white. Balls are drawn from the box one after the other, without being replaced, until both the white balls are picked. If the second ball picked is white, determine the probability that exactly 4 balls were picked out of the box. ( ) 9\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Imagine the complete random order of the five balls: [B1] [A1] Conditional on position 2 being white, the other white ball is equally likely to occupy position 1, 3, 4 or 5. [M1] [A1] Exactly four balls are drawn if and only if this second white position is 4. [B1] [A1] For the full stopping-time tree let W denote white and B any other colour: Stop the first time the second W appears: A representative path for each length and its possible orders are length representative path path probability number of paths 2 WW (2/5)(1/4) = 1/10 1 3 BWW (3/5)(2/4)(1/3) = 1/10 2 4 BWBW (3/5)(2/4)(2/3)(1/2) = 1/10 3 5 BWBBW (3/5)(2/4)(2/3)(1/2)(1) = 1/10 4 In each row the last draw is white and the earlier white can occupy any preceding position: In the source enumeration the possible white positions, given a white in position 2, are {1, 2}, {2, 3}, {2, 4}, {2, 5}.  = 1/10. 5 Each unordered choice of two positions before conditioning has probability 1/ 2\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The ratio for the third case is (1/10)/(4/10). 1 The probability is 4 . [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:290.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
