    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-t/q4",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q4-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A multiple choice paper has n questions, where n &gt; 20 . Each question has 5 options of which only 1 is correct. A pass is obtained if at least 20 questions are answered correctly. The probability of obtaining a pass by randomly guessing the answers is less than 2.5% . ( 12 ) By using a distributional approximation, calculate the least value of n .\" style=\"--madas-width:578.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Under independent random guesses, X ∼B(n, 0.2). [B1] [A1] Approximate by Y ∼N(0.2n, 0.16n). [M1] [A1] With a continuity correction, P(X ≥20) ≈P(Y &gt; 19.5). [A1] For the upper tail to be below 0.025, solve 19.5 −0.2n &gt; Φ−1(0.975) ≈1.95996. 0.4√n [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Multiply through by 0.4√n &gt; 0 to get 19.5 −0.2n &gt; 0.783985√n, then move the variable terms to one side and divide by 0.2. Putting z = √n [A1] gives z2 + 3.91993z −97.5 &lt; 0, [A1] hence n &lt; 65.72 . . .. [A1] The largest integer under this approximation is 65 . [M1] [A1] Source correction. The printed question asks for the least n, which would be 21 under its stated condition: The worked scan solves for the greatest n using the normal approximation: The exact binomial cutoff would be 64.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A multiple choice paper has n questions, where n > 20 . Each question has 5 options of which only 1 is correct. A pass is obtained if at least 20 questions are answered correctly. The probability of obtaining a pass by randomly guessing the answers is less than 2.5% . ( 12 ) By using a distributional approximation, calculate the least value of n ."
