    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-t/q9",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Two forces, both of magnitude 5 N each, have a resultant of magnitude 8 N . These two forces act on a particle, of mass m kg , which remains at rest on a smooth horizontal surface. The surface makes an acute angle θ with one of the 5 N forces. Given that the surface exerts a force of  4 N to the particle determine the exact value of m and the exact value of cosθ . You may not use any calculating aid in this question. ( 10 )\" style=\"--madas-width:581.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let ϕ be the included angle in the triangle formed by the two 5 N forces and their 8 N resultant. The cosine rule gives 82 = 52 + 52 −2(5)(5) cos ϕ. [B1] Hence cos ϕ = −14/50 = −7/25 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The angle ψ between the forces drawn from a common point is supplementary to ϕ, so cos ψ = 7/25. [B1] In equilibrium the two 5 N forces must be symmetrical about the vertical: They point away from the plane since a downward resultant of 8 N could not be balanced by the 4 N reaction and a positive weight: [A1] Use cos ψ = 2 cos2(ψ/2) −1. [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q9-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Thus cos2(ψ/2) = 16/25, and the acute half-angle gives cos(ψ/2) = 4/5. [A1] Rearranging the half-angle identity gives 2 cos2(ψ/2) = 1 + 7/25 . = 32/25 Each tension contributes 5(4/5) = 4 N vertically and their horizontal components cancel: Vertical equilibrium gives mg = 2(5) cos(ψ/2) + 4. [A1] Substituting gives mg = 8 + 4 = 12, hence m = 60/49 . [A1] Since θ + ψ/2 = 90◦, use the right triangle with hypotenuse 5, adjacent side 4 for ψ/2, and remaining side 3. [M1] Hence cos θ = 3/5 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 9 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "Two forces, both of magnitude 5 N each, have a resultant of magnitude 8 N . These two forces act on a particle, of mass m kg , which remains at rest on a smooth horizontal surface. The surface makes an acute angle θ with one of the 5 N forces. Given that the surface exerts a force of  4 N to the particle determine the exact value of m and the exact value of cosθ . You may not use any calculating aid in this question. ( 10 )"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-t/q10",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q10-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A P 60° R B\" style=\"--madas-width:489.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q10-question-2.svg\" alt=\"A light inextensible string is threaded through a ring R , of weight W . The two ends of the string, A and B , are attached on a wall with A vertically above B . The ring is in equilibrium by a force P acting on R , so that BR is horizontal with ∡ BRA = 60 ° , as shown in the figure above. Determine, in terms of W , the magnitude of P , when the tension in the string is least. ( ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The two equal tensions have resultant of magnitude S = 2T cos 30◦, [M1] [A1] directed 30◦above the leftward horizontal: [A1] The applied force must equal the vector difference between the vertically upward force of magnitude W and this resultant. [A1] The closest point on the resultant’s ray to the tip of the upward vector is its perpendicular projection: [A1] The angle between the upward vector and the ray is 60◦. [A1] At the perpendicular projection S = W cos 60◦\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-t/q10-solution-2.svg\" alt=\". = W/2 The residual vector is perpendicular to S, giving a right triangle with hypotenuse W and opposite side P. Thus the minimum force is √ W sin 60◦= 3 2 W . [M1] [A1] Source correction. The printed wording asks for P when the tension is least, while the handwritten working finds the minimum P. With a freely chosen force direction the tension approaches zero and P approaches W. The calculation above follows the handwritten minimization of P.\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 10 is 8 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A P 60° R B A light inextensible string is threaded through a ring R , of weight W . The two ends of the string, A and B , are attached on a wall with A vertically above B . The ring is in equilibrium by a force P acting on R , so that BR is horizontal with ∡ BRA = 60 ° , as shown in the figure above. Determine, in terms of W , the magnitude of P , when the tension in the string is least. ( ) 8"
